.TH FRAME 2
.SH NAME
frame: parse, solve, memberforces, equilibrium \- plane frames and trusses
.SH SYNOPSIS
.EX
include "frame.m";
frame := load Frame Frame->PATH;
Struct, Member, Node, Forces: import frame;

frame->init();
(s, err) := frame->parse(spec);
err = frame->solve(s);
v := s.displacement("c", Frame->DY);
r := s.reactionat("a", Frame->DY);
f := frame->memberforces(s, 0);
(fx, fy, mo) := frame->equilibrium(s);
.EE
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Frame
solves plane frames and trusses by the direct stiffness method.
The structure is written down the way a statics problem is set out \-
where the joints are, what runs between them, what holds it up, and what
pushes on it:
.IP
.EX
node a 0 0
node b 4 0
node c 4 3
beam a b 200e9 0.01 1e-5
bar  b c 200e9 0.005
pin a
roller b y
force c 0 -1000
.EE
.PP
A
.B node
is a name and two coordinates.
A
.B beam
is two nodes, Young's modulus, the area and the second moment of area; a
.B bar
is the same without the second moment, which is what pin-jointed means.
.B Udl
on the line after a member puts a uniformly distributed load across it,
positive to the member's left.
.PP
.B Fix
holds a node completely,
.B pin
holds it in place but lets it turn, and
.B roller
.I node
.RB [ x | y ]
holds one direction only.
.B Force
is two components at a node and
.B moment
is one.
.PP
.B UNITS ARE THE CALLER'S
and must be consistent \- newtons and metres throughout, or pounds and
inches throughout.
Nothing here converts anything, and a structure given
.I E
in gigapascals and the area in square millimetres returns a deflection
wrong by a factor that no check can notice.
.PP
Every node has three degrees of freedom \- across, up, and rotation \-
so one element does both jobs.
.B Solve
assembles the stiffness matrix, removes the held degrees of freedom and
factors what is left with
.IR linalg (2).
.B Displacement
and
.B reactionat
read the answer by node name and direction;
.B memberforces
gives one member's axial force (tension positive) and the shear and
moment at each of its ends, in the member's own directions.
.PP
.B Equilibrium
returns the residual of the three balance equations \- the sum of the
sideways forces, of the vertical forces, and of the moments about the
origin \- each of which must be zero, and which is the check every
statics answer has to pass.
.SS Rotations with nothing to resist them
A joint where only bars meet has nothing resisting its rotation.
That degree of freedom is
.B held
.B at
.B zero
rather than left to make the matrix singular, and
.B Struct.heldrot
counts how many were.
This is a decision about the model and not an implementation detail,
which is why it is said out loud and counted.
.PP
A
.B translation
with nothing resisting it is a different matter and is
.BR refused :
holding it would invent a support the structure does not have and return
a confident answer about a different structure.
A single pin-jointed bar with a free end is a pendulum, and
.I frame
says so, even when the load on it is purely along the bar \- in which
case the matrix is not singular and nothing else would catch it.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/lib/frame.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR linalg (2),
.IR fem (2),
.IR femesh (2),
.IR frametest (1)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.B Parse
and
.B solve
return a diagnostic string, nil on success.
A structure that is a mechanism \- some part of which moves without
stretching anything \- is refused, naming the joint and the direction
where that is possible.
That is a real answer about the structure, not a numerical failure.
.SH BUGS
Plane structures only.
There is no space frame, no torsion, and no out-of-plane bending.
.PP
Small displacements and linear elasticity throughout.
There is no buckling check, so a member returned with a large
compressive force may in reality have failed long before reaching it,
and nothing here will say so.
.PP
Loads are applied at nodes, apart from
.BR udl ,
so a point load partway along a member needs a node put there.
The element is exact at its nodes, so that costs nothing in accuracy \-
but the deflection
.I between
nodes is not reported at all.
.PP
There is no self-weight: a member's mass is not used, and
.B udl
must be written out for it.
